      '<div class="learn-intro"><span class="learn-keyword">Gaseous exchange</span> is the process by which organisms take in oxygen (for respiration) and release carbon dioxide (a waste product). Exchange surfaces are adapted for efficiency in different organisms.</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Respiratory Surfaces: Adaptations</h3><p class="learn-p">All efficient gaseous exchange surfaces share:</p><ul class="learn-list"><li>Large surface area (to maximise diffusion rate)</li><li>Thin walls (short diffusion distance)</li><li>Moist surface (gases dissolve in moisture for diffusion)</li><li>Rich blood supply (maintains concentration gradient)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Gaseous Exchange in Different Organisms</h3><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Organism</th><th>Exchange Surface</th><th>Adaptation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fish</td><td>Gills</td><td>Gill filaments and lamellae; counter-current flow maximises O₂ uptake</td></tr><tr><td>Insects</td><td>Tracheae/spiracles</td><td>Air tubes deliver O₂ directly to cells; no blood used for gas transport</td></tr><tr><td>Earthworm</td><td>Moist skin</td><td>Gases diffuse through moist mucus-covered body surface</td></tr><tr><td>Frogs</td><td>Lungs + moist skin</td><td>Both respiratory surfaces used</td></tr><tr><td>Humans</td><td>Alveoli in lungs</td><td>300 million alveoli; vast surface area ~70 m²</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Human Respiratory System</h3><p class="learn-p">Pathway: nose → trachea → bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli. Alveoli adaptations: thin walls (one cell), moist surface, surrounded by capillaries, large number (huge SA).</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Cellular Respiration</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li><strong>Aerobic:</strong> C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + 38 ATP (in mitochondria)</li><li><strong>Anaerobic in animals:</strong> glucose → lactic acid + 2 ATP (no O₂)</li><li><strong>Anaerobic in yeast:</strong> glucose → ethanol + CO₂ + 2 ATP (fermentation)</li></ul><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>Aerobic vs Anaerobic:</strong> Aerobic = 38 ATP, complete oxidation, needs O₂. Anaerobic = 2 ATP, incomplete, no O₂ needed, produces lactic acid (animals) or ethanol+CO₂ (yeast).</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Exchange surface: large SA, thin, moist, good blood supply. Alveoli (humans), gills (fish), tracheae (insects). Aerobic: 38 ATP. Anaerobic: 2 ATP + lactic acid or ethanol.</div>',
