    contentHTML:
      '<div class="learn-intro"><span class="learn-keyword">Reproduction</span> ensures the continuation of a species. Plants reproduce sexually (involving flowers, pollination, fertilisation, and seed dispersal) and asexually (vegetative propagation).</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Structure of a Flower</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li><strong>Sepals:</strong> protect the bud</li><li><strong>Petals:</strong> attract pollinators (coloured, scented)</li><li><strong>Stamens:</strong> male organs — anther (produces pollen) + filament</li><li><strong>Carpel/Pistil:</strong> female organs — stigma (receives pollen) + style + ovary (contains ovules)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Pollination</h3><div class="learn-table-wrap"><table class="learn-table"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Wind-pollinated (anemophily)</th><th>Insect-pollinated (entomophily)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Petals</td><td>Small, green/absent</td><td>Large, brightly coloured</td></tr><tr><td>Pollen</td><td>Light, smooth, abundant</td><td>Sticky, spiny, less abundant</td></tr><tr><td>Stigma</td><td>Feathery, outside flower</td><td>Sticky, inside flower</td></tr><tr><td>Nectar/scent</td><td>Absent</td><td>Present</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Maize, grasses</td><td>Hibiscus, sunflower</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Fertilisation and Seed Development</h3><p class="learn-p">Double fertilisation in flowering plants: pollen grain germinates on stigma → pollen tube grows down style → two male nuclei enter ovule. One fertilises the egg cell (→ zygote → embryo). The other fuses with polar nuclei (→ triploid endosperm). Zygote → embryo; ovule → seed; ovary wall → fruit.</p><h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Seed Dispersal</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li><strong>Wind:</strong> light seeds with wings/parachutes (dandelion, maple)</li><li><strong>Water:</strong> buoyant seeds (coconut)</li><li><strong>Animals:</strong> hooks/spines (burrs) or fleshy fruits eaten and seeds excreted</li><li><strong>Explosive mechanisms:</strong> seed pods burst under tension (peas, beans)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Vegetative Propagation</h3><p class="learn-p">Asexual reproduction: runners (strawberry), tubers (yam, potato), bulbs (onion, garlic), rhizomes (ginger), suckers (banana). Advantages: faster, identical offspring; Disadvantages: no genetic variation.</p><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>Double Fertilisation:</strong> Male nucleus 1 + egg = zygote (embryo). Male nucleus 2 + 2 polar nuclei = endosperm (food for embryo). Unique to flowering plants.</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Flower: sepal, petal, stamen (anther+filament), carpel (stigma+style+ovary). Pollination: wind (light pollen) or insect (sticky pollen). Double fertilisation. Ovule→seed; ovary→fruit.</div>',
