      '<div class="learn-intro"><span class="learn-keyword">Behaviour</span> refers to all the actions and responses of an organism. Animal behaviour can be innate (inherited, not learned) or learned (acquired through experience). Behaviour helps animals survive and reproduce in their environment.</div><h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Innate (Instinctive) Behaviour</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li>Present from birth; genetically determined; no learning required</li><li>Examples: reflex actions (withdrawal from pain), fixed action patterns (FAPs), taxis, kinesis, migration in many animals</li><li><strong>Taxis:</strong> directed movement toward (+) or away from (-) a stimulus. Phototaxis (light), chemotaxis (chemicals), thigmotaxis (contact)</li><li><strong>Kinesis:</strong> undirected change in speed of movement in response to a stimulus intensity (not directional)</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Learned Behaviour</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li><strong>Habituation:</strong> organism stops responding to a repeated harmless stimulus. Example: garden snail withdrawing and re-emerging after repeated touches.</li><li><strong>Imprinting:</strong> learning during a critical period early in life. Example: newly hatched goslings following the first moving object they see (Lorenz\'s experiments).</li><li><strong>Classical conditioning:</strong> association of a neutral stimulus with a natural stimulus. Example: Pavlov\'s dogs salivating at a bell.</li><li><strong>Operant conditioning:</strong> learning through trial and error and reinforcement. Example: rat pressing a lever for food (Skinner box).</li><li><strong>Insight learning:</strong> solving a novel problem without trial and error. Example: chimpanzees using sticks to reach bananas.</li></ul><h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Social Behaviour</h3><ul class="learn-list"><li><strong>Courtship:</strong> behaviours that attract a mate and ensure successful mating (display, songs, scent)</li><li><strong>Territorial behaviour:</strong> defence of an area providing resources (food, mates, nesting sites)</li><li><strong>Dominance hierarchies:</strong> social ranking determining access to resources (pecking order)</li><li><strong>Communication:</strong> visual (colour, display), acoustic (calls, songs), chemical (pheromones)</li></ul><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span><strong>Taxis vs Kinesis:</strong> Taxis = DIRECTED movement toward or away from stimulus. Kinesis = UNDIRECTED change in movement rate in response to stimulus intensity.</div><div class="learn-tip-box"><span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Innate: reflex, taxis (directed), kinesis (undirected). Learned: habituation, imprinting, classical conditioning (Pavlov), operant (Skinner), insight (chimps). Social: courtship, territory, dominance, pheromones.</div>',
